> Not bad! The code looks rather clean.
>
> Is --ondisk in .wks being locked to a specific SD card device?
=C2=A0
Yes and no. IIRC you need to pass a disk name that the partition would
reside on. This is only meaningful when partitions listed in kickstart end =
up
being listed by wic in fstab as well. In this case, fstab was not
generated by wic, hence disk name is in fact redundant. I'll check if it is
possible to skip the disk name completely, but I think it would be confusin=
g
if we diverged too far from regular kickstart syntax.

>
> And in general - this can be extended and re-used for other platforms in
> meta-ti, not just BeagleBone.

I guess this is the intention of OE guys. I think that effectively, it'd be
better if there was a recipe that prepared a directory with contents of
the boot partition in some well known location within the work area plus
a couple of bootimg source plugins could generate the partition images
that cover most use cases (i.e. single vfat partition with u-boot; u-boot
right after MBR + vfat partition with kernel; extra bootloader + u-boot
+ whatever).

The patch is a kind of a PoC, I'll do some cleanups, check on the disk
name and post an updated version. At the same time, these
https://www.yoctoproject.org/downloads/bsps/daisy16/beaglebone are the only
instructions for getting a SD card image. I'd be great if this could be mad=
e
simpler.

I'm trying to prepare a similar setup for some of the meta-fsl boards
(they actually have their own image class) and meta-raspberrypi (again,
own IMAGE_FSTYPE - rpi-sdimg).


